# LORE self-hosting settings. Every line is optional: `docker compose up -d`
# works with no .env at all. To change something, copy this file to .env
# next to docker-compose.yml, uncomment the line and edit it, then run
# `docker compose up -d` again. Guide: docs/self-hosting.md

# --- Where LORE answers --------------------------------------------------------

# The port on this computer (http://localhost:8080 by default).
# LORE_PORT=8080

# Which of this computer's addresses to listen on. 0.0.0.0: every address, so
# other devices on your network can reach it. 127.0.0.1: this computer only.
# LORE_BIND=0.0.0.0

# The address people open LORE at. Set it when that is not
# http://localhost:<LORE_PORT>, for example a LAN name or your HTTPS domain:
# set-password links are built from it, and LORE sends Secure cookies exactly
# when it starts with https://.
# LORE_BASE_URL=https://lore.example.com

# Host names LORE answers besides localhost, IP addresses and LORE_BASE_URL's
# host (comma-separated; `*` answers any name). For example a name your router
# gives this machine:
# LORE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=nas.local,lore.home.arpa

# Force Secure cookies on (1) or off (0). By default they follow
# LORE_BASE_URL: on for https://, off for http:// (so sign-in works on
# http://localhost and on a LAN address).
# LORE_COOKIE_SECURE=

# Behind your own reverse proxy (Caddy, nginx, Traefik): 1 takes the client's
# address from X-Forwarded-For, for the sign-in rate limit.
# LORE_TRUST_PROXY=0

# --- Embeddings (Ollama) ---------------------------------------------------------

# By default the bundled `ollama` service embeds text with nomic-embed-text,
# pulled automatically on first start. To use an Ollama you already run, point
# LORE at it; the bundled one then idles. On this same computer (Ollama must
# listen beyond 127.0.0.1: OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 ollama serve):
# LORE_OLLAMA_HOST=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# On another machine:
# LORE_OLLAMA_HOST=http://192.168.1.50:11434

# --- The AI model ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Chosen in the web app, not here: Admin page -> "AI provider for everyone"
# (Gemini, Claude, OpenAI with a key, or a local model). A llama.cpp
# llama-server on this computer, started with `--host 0.0.0.0 --port 8081`,
# is the address http://host.docker.internal:8081/v1 there.

# --- The image ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which prebuilt image to run (`docker compose pull` fetches it). A version
# tag such as 0.2.0 pins it; `edge` follows the main branch.
# LORE_VERSION=latest
# LORE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/jatin-roopchandani/lore
# OLLAMA_VERSION=latest

# --- Data ------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything LORE keeps (libraries, the account database, the secrets) is in
# the `lore-data` volume. Back it up: see docs/self-hosting.md.
#
# The account database is a SQLite file in that volume. To use a Postgres
# server you run instead (an empty database; LORE creates its tables):
# LORE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://lore:password@db.example:5432/lore

# --- Secrets ------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Made on first start and kept in the volume (/data/secrets.env). Set them
# here only to manage them yourself. Never change LORE_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY on
# its own: the saved API keys are sealed with it.
# LORE_SESSION_SECRET=
# LORE_CSRF_SECRET=
# LORE_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
